  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "$Γ(\\frac{7}{2})+Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=$",
  "worked_solution": "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nΓ(\\frac{7}{2})+Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }+\\sqrt{\\pi }=\\frac{23}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }.\n$$",
